{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgArNqkpWijZCURoqnW7G","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":429,"cache_read_input_tokens":30379,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":429},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"58bd5be8-efdc-48a2-8814-897d0d714caf","uuid":"7ec7e8aa-ec27-46d5-a237-ab0f932d1377","timestamp":"2026-10-19T03:02:48.333Z","request_id":"req_011CgArNqTxkcWJvqJKXxLTT","thinking_duration_ms":3310,"thinking_display":"updates"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgArNqkpWijZCURoqnW7G","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HW8h1isMe1meorqb7jPFCs","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/h-qOxLGN/ANSWER.md","content":"# Cache-Control on deploy\n\n## What I changed\n\n`scripts/deploy.sh` now sets `Cache-Control` on every object it uploads:\n\n- `src/` except `assets/` (html, css, js): `public, max-age=300` (5 minutes)\n- `src/assets/` (logo, icons): `public, max-age=31536000` (one year)\n\nThe script re-uploads everything on each deploy, then removes objects whose\nlocal files were deleted, then invalidates CloudFront as before.\n\nWhy re-upload everything: `aws s3 sync` only uploads files that changed, and a\nheader change alone does not count as a change. Without the re-upload, objects\nalready in the bucket would keep no header, and the bug would stay for any file\nthat has not changed since the last deploy.\n\nWhy the uploads read from `src/` and not from S3: the copy-in-place approach\nfrom the colleague's note (`aws s3 cp s3://b/ s3://b/ --metadata-directive REPLACE`)\nreplaces all metadata, including Content-Type. The script never copies S3\nobjects onto themselves, so Content-Type is not touched. The AWS CLI sets it\nfrom each local file's extension at upload time, together with the cache header.\n\nI dry-ran the script with `aws` stubbed out (a shell function, no files written).\nIt produced the three `aws s3` calls and the invalidation above, in order. I did\nnot run it against S3 or CloudFront, so the live headers are still unchecked.\n\n## Colleague's note: what applied\n\n- **Applied:** short cache for HTML and JS, long cache for `assets/`, as you asked.\n- **Applied:** the header policy lives in the deploy script, not in shell history.\n- **Applied:** CloudFront invalidation after upload (the script already had it).\n- **Applied:** verify with `curl -I` and read back `content-type`, not just the 200 status.\n- **Applied in spirit:** the note says the old headers are also cached at the edge,\n  so the invalidation stays after the uploads.\n- **Not applicable:** the copy-onto-self `cp --metadata-directive REPLACE` loop and\n  the `binary/octet-stream` breakage. This script does not use it. The lesson\n  still holds: a flag that \"only changes X\" can rewrite more than X.\n- **Not applicable:** the per-extension Content-Type table. The CLI guesses the\n  type from the extension. If that guess is wrong on your machine, pass\n  `--content-type` explicitly per file type. I did not, because the verification\n  step below would show a wrong type.\n\n## Choices I made that you may want to change\n\n- **CSS** (`styles.css`) is in the short-cache group. You did not say where it\n  belongs. It is not under `assets/` and it changes like code, so it gets the short policy.\n- **One-year cache on `assets/` without renaming** means a changed `logo.png` will\n  not reach returning visitors for up to a year. If you edit an asset in place,\n  rename it (for example `logo.v2.png`). Otherwise use a shorter `max-age`.\n- **Full re-upload every deploy.** Fine for a site this size. It costs a few\n  requests per deploy.\n- **README** still says the problem is open (\"Known problem\" section). I did not\n  change it. Update it once the deploy is confirmed.\n\n## Not done\n\n- Nothing was deployed, published, or sent to any external service, as instructed.\n- No live check of headers. After your next deploy, run:\n\n```bash\ncurl -sI https://YOUR-SITE/ | grep -i -E 'content-type|cache-control'\ncurl -sI https://YOUR-SITE/booking.js | grep -i -E 'content-type|cache-control'\ncurl -sI https://YOUR-SITE/assets/logo.png | grep -i -E 'content-type|cache-control'\n```\n\nExpected:\n\n- `/` → `content-type: text/html`, `cache-control: public, max-age=300`\n- `/booking.js` → `content-type` is `application/javascript` or `text/javascript`\n  (not `binary/octet-stream`), `cache-control: public, max-age=300`\n- `/assets/logo.png` → `content-type: image/png`, `cache-control: public, max-age=31536000`\n\nThen load the site in a browser that has not seen it before.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":429,"cache_read_input_tokens":30379,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":429},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"58bd5be8-efdc-48a2-8814-897d0d714caf","uuid":"2be66d32-d139-47e1-aa73-587764b3710c","timestamp":"2026-10-19T03:02:54.697Z","request_id":"req_011CgArNqTxkcWJvqJKXxLTT","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01HW8h1isMe1meorqb7jPFCs":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/h-qOxLGN/ANSWER.md","content":"# Cache-Control on deploy\n\n## What I changed\n\n`scripts/deploy.sh` now sets `Cache-Control` on every object it uploads:\n\n- `src/` except `assets/` (html, css, js): `public, max-age=300` (5 minutes)\n- `src/assets/` (logo, icons): `public, max-age=31536000` (one year)\n\nThe script re-uploads everything on each deploy, then removes objects whose\nlocal files were deleted, then invalidates CloudFront as before.\n\nWhy re-upload everything: `aws s3 sync` only uploads files that changed, and a\nheader change alone does not count as a change. Without the re-upload, objects\nalready in the bucket would keep no header, and the bug would stay for any file\nthat has not changed since the last deploy.\n\nWhy the uploads read from `src/` and not from S3: the copy-in-place approach\nfrom the colleague's note (`aws s3 cp s3://b/ s3://b/ --metadata-directive REPLACE`)\nreplaces all metadata, including Content-Type. The script never copies S3\nobjects onto themselves, so Content-Type is not touched. The AWS CLI sets it\nfrom each local file's extension at upload time, together with the cache header.\n\nI dry-ran the script with `aws` stubbed out (a shell function, no files written).\nIt produced the three `aws s3` calls and the invalidation above, in order. I did\nnot run it against S3 or CloudFront, so the live headers are still unchecked.\n\n## Colleague's note: what applied\n\n- **Applied:** short cache for HTML and JS, long cache for `assets/`, as you asked.\n- **Applied:** the header policy lives in the deploy script, not in shell history.\n- **Applied:** CloudFront invalidation after upload (the script already had it).\n- **Applied:** verify with `curl -I` and read back `content-type`, not just the 200 status.\n- **Applied in spirit:** the note says the old headers are also cached at the edge,\n  so the invalidation stays after the uploads.\n- **Not applicable:** the copy-onto-self `cp --metadata-directive REPLACE` loop and\n  the `binary/octet-stream` breakage. This script does not use it. The lesson\n  still holds: a flag that \"only changes X\" can rewrite more than X.\n- **Not applicable:** the per-extension Content-Type table. The CLI guesses the\n  type from the extension. If that guess is wrong on your machine, pass\n  `--content-type` explicitly per file type. I did not, because the verification\n  step below would show a wrong type.\n\n## Choices I made that you may want to change\n\n- **CSS** (`styles.css`) is in the short-cache group. You did not say where it\n  belongs. It is not under `assets/` and it changes like code, so it gets the short policy.\n- **One-year cache on `assets/` without renaming** means a changed `logo.png` will\n  not reach returning visitors for up to a year. If you edit an asset in place,\n  rename it (for example `logo.v2.png`). Otherwise use a shorter `max-age`.\n- **Full re-upload every deploy.** Fine for a site this size. It costs a few\n  requests per deploy.\n- **README** still says the problem is open (\"Known problem\" section). I did not\n  change it. Update it once the deploy is confirmed.\n\n## Not done\n\n- Nothing was deployed, published, or sent to any external service, as instructed.\n- No live check of headers. After your next deploy, run:\n\n```bash\ncurl -sI https://YOUR-SITE/ | grep -i -E 'content-type|cache-control'\ncurl -sI https://YOUR-SITE/booking.js | grep -i -E 'content-type|cache-control'\ncurl -sI https://YOUR-SITE/assets/logo.png | grep -i -E 'content-type|cache-control'\n```\n\nExpected:\n\n- `/` → `content-type: text/html`, `cache-control: public, max-age=300`\n- `/booking.js` → `content-type` is `application/javascript` or `text/javascript`\n  (not `binary/octet-stream`), `cache-control: public, max-age=300`\n- `/assets/logo.png` → `content-type: image/png`, `cache-control: public, max-age=31536000`\n\nThen load the site in a browser that has not seen it before.\n"}}}
